NNAAMMEE
     msg - send a brief message to other users

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     mmssgg _u_s_e_r
     mmssgg _u_s_e_r ... _m_e_s_s_a_g_e

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     In the first form, mmssgg reads one line from the standard in-
     put and sends it to _u_s_e_r. In the second form, mmssgg sends the
     final argument, _m_e_s_s_a_g_e, to every named _u_s_e_r in the
     preceding list; if _m_e_s_s_a_g_e is -, mmssgg instead reads the mes-
     sage as one line from the standard input.

     For each named _u_s_e_r, mmssgg searches /eettcc/uuttmmpp to find the ter-
     minal on which _u_s_e_r is logged in, then writes the message to
     that terminal, prefixed with the sender's login name (or
     ddaaeemmoonn if the sender is not logged in).  If a named _u_s_e_r is
     not currently logged in, mmssgg prints "_u_s_e_r: not logged in"
     and, unless invoked with -ss, sets a nonzero exit status.

     Before writing to a terminal, mmssgg checks whether the
     group/other execute bit of that terminal's device file is
     set (the state controlled by mmeessgg); if it is not, an ordi-
     nary caller is refused, but the superuser is asked whether
     to override the refusal.  A write that does not complete
     within ten seconds is abandoned, so mmssgg cannot hang waiting
     on a terminal that is not reading its input.

     If the caller is the superuser and the first argument is -ss,
     mmssgg suppresses the sender's login-name prefix on the mes-
     sage, closes its standard error (so no diagnostics are
     printed), and forces a zero exit status unless a real error
     (such as being unable to open /eettcc/uuttmmpp) occurs.  This is
     intended for use by programs, such as rrmmaaiill, that relay mail
     notifications through mmssgg, and has no effect for an ordinary
     caller.

FFIILLEESS
     /eettcc/uuttmmpp

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     mmeessgg, wwrriittee

DDIIAAGGNNOOSSTTIICCSS
     mmssgg prints its usage message and exits with status 2 if
     called with no arguments, exits with status 2 if /eettcc/uuttmmpp
     cannot be opened, and otherwise exits with status 1 if any
     named user could not be reached (unless -ss was given).

NNOOTTEESS
     mmssgg is normally installed set-user-id, so that its execute
     permission on a terminal device can be used to allow or dis-
     allow use of the terminal by other users, without granting
     general write access to device files.


